(1) Initial adsorption in MOFs: Gas infusion into MOFs is the most common process in all gas-related applications. However, the gas kinetics in the initial $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption are still unclear. To capture the instantaneous changes at the early stage, the gas flow rate should be as slow as possible. Herein, the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ flow rate was regulated as approximately $\mathbf{o.4mm^{3}/s e c}$ . The studied objects, PCN$_{30}\mathrm{X}$ and PCN-12, have $^3$ -fold window openings (Figure 7c), which will be the focus in this section.
Interpreted from the differences between the reference and the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -loaded crystals, a molecular transport path was observed between the coordinately unsaturated metal units and the window sites. In Figure 5 measured at $295\mathrm{K}.$ , the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules approached the open $\mathtt{C u}$ sites along the $c$ -axis at the very beginning (Figure 5a), followed with the increasing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ population at the open Cu sites and in the window pockets (Figure $5\mathrm{b}$ ). Successively, the major portion of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules migrated30 from the open Cu sites to the window pockets (Figure $\mathrm{5c})$ ), completing a “kinetic transition”. This indicates that the open metal sites perform as kinetic first attractors that direct $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules to thermodynamically stable adopters.31 Also, the first docked molecule is a directing agent of subsequent molecules, as it facilitates the sorption to spread throughout the whole structure starting from the open metal sites.32
This phenomenon of kinetic transition did not occur only at room temperature; it also happened near the condensation temperature of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (S.I. 3.2.5). At 150K, a small amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules were attracted to the open $\mathrm{cu}$ sites at the onset. In seconds, the incoming $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules navigated to the adjacent cages (Figure 2c) rather than moving into the window pockets, reaching another thermodynamically favored site. With continuous $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ addition, the large pores were gradually filled along the $a$ - axis of the crystal (Figure 11c). The temperature effect on the final states of gas migration will be discussed in section 3 describing gas redistribution with temperature change. Although the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ destinations at the two temperatures $(295/150\mathrm{K})$ were different, both of the surface/volume ratios of the observed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ electron clouds decreased with loading time (Table 1). This displays a visible tendency of surface minimization upon $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ infusion. The parallel results of PCN-307 can be found in S.I. 3.3.5. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first example of experimental evidence of the kinetic transition of gas molecules in MOFs.
In order to deeply study the kinetic transition from open $\boldsymbol{C u}$ sites to other structural spaces, further analysis was done on PCN-12. In addition to the $^3$ -fold window sites (Figure 7c), PCN-12 is also constructed by larger $4^{-}$ fold openings (Figure 7b), and there are various sizes of cages in the structure. As shown in Figure 7a, PCN-12 comprises four Wyckoff positions of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ units. As viewed along the $c$ -axis, the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ units labeled with ${8s/4\bf{k}}$ as well as those with $8\mathrm{r}/40$ build identical symmetrical cages 11 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ wide. The $8s/40$ units form large ellipsoidal cages $17\mathring\mathrm{A}$ long, and the $\mathrm{8r/4k}$ units form small cavities $9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ wide. The cages constructed by different sets of open metal sites were not equivalently accessible for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules. The cages consisting of $\mathrm{8r/40}$ sets and ${8s/4\bf{k}}$ sets were populated with $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ whereas those with the $8s/40$ and $\mathrm{8r/4k}$ sets were left vacant. This nonequivalence of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ amounts on these open metal sites indicates that gas molecules discriminate among the binding sites in favor of those with nearby multipoint interactions4c and appropriate spatial accessibility.
Another kinetic transition occurred on this adsorbent. In Figure 6a, the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules were trapped by the open $\mathtt{C u}$ sites 4o and the $^4$ -fold window openings, both of which are the most easily accessible regions on the central channels. In Figure 6b, the transition direction changed from the open $\mathtt{C u}$ sites (Wyckoff 4o) to the larger $^4$ -fold window openings but not the smaller $_3$ -fold sites. The DED contours showed a close ring connecting the area between the open $\mathtt{C u}$ sites and their bordering 4- fold window openings. Further $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ loading did not diffuse homogenously into the small volume, which contains the available open $\mathtt{C u}$ sites classified as 4k. Instead, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules aggregated in the center of the channels to reach the surface minimum (Figure 6c and Table 1).
Consequently, this time-dependent kinetic transition can be recognized at various temperatures and in different adsorbents. Especially for Figure $5\mathsf{b-c},\mathsf{\beta-}33$ the transition continues at the near equilibrium state. We attribute this unusual behavior, molecular transport from one binding site to another, to an inevitable consequence of thermodynamics. Based on the basic thermodynamic relationship,
$$
\Delta\mathrm{G}=\Delta\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{T}\Delta\mathrm{S}
$$
the transition process will be spontaneous $\left(\Delta\mathsf{G}<0\right)$ if one of the following conditions are true: a $\Delta\mathrm{H}<0$ , $\Delta S>0$ , b $\Delta\mathrm{H}<0$ , $\Delta S<0$ , plus $\left|\mathrm{T}\Delta\mathrm{S}\right|<\left|\Delta\mathrm{H}\right|$ or c $\Delta\mathrm{H}>0$ , $\Delta S>0$ , plus $\left|\mathrm{T}\Delta\mathrm{S}\right|>\left|\Delta\mathrm{H}\right|$ where $\Delta\mathrm{H}<0$ is defined as exothermic heat, and $\Delta S<0$ refers to the transition to fewer molecular degrees of freedom in the process. According to Wu et al.’s report,5a the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ binding energy of open metal sites is around 25 $\mathrm{{kJ/mol}}.$ ., roughly identical to that of window sites.34 This means that $\left|\Delta\mathrm{H}\right|$ is extremely small. We attribute this discovery to (c); that is, the gas molecules will be able to break away from the potential well if ${\mathrm{T}}\Delta{\mathrm{S}}\geq\Delta{\mathrm{H}}\approx0$ . More precisely, we made an effort to investigate how this entropic driving force pushes gas molecules out of the potential well in the equilibrium states. A localized-tomobile model,35 considering the adsorbates partially localized and partially mobile, was hypothesized to fit our observations.
In this model simplified with statistical treatment35a (details in S.I. sec.5), $f$ is introduced to define the maximum fraction of the adsorbent surfaces on which adsorbates can be localized. $\theta_{l}$ and $\theta_{m}$ are the fractions of truly adsorbed molecules on the localized and mobile areas, respectively. $N_{s l}$ , $N_{l}$ and $N_{m}$ are the molecular numbers corresponding to $f,\ \theta_{l}$ and $\theta_{m}$ . The relationship between the above parameters can be expressed as
$$
f=N_{s l}/N_{s},\quad\theta_{m}=N_{m}/N_{s},\quad\theta_{l}=N_{l}/N_{s},\quad\theta=\theta_{l}+\theta_{m}
$$
where $N_{s}$ is the maximum number of the molecules that can be adsorbed on all solid surfaces. The partition functions for a localized molecule $(q_{l})$ and for a mobile molecule $(q_{m})$ are
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{q_{l}=q_{v x}q_{v y}q_{v z}\displaystyle\exp\left(-\frac{u_{p l}}{k T}\right)}}\\ {{q_{m}=\displaystyle\left(\frac{2\pi m k T}{h^{2}}\right)q_{v z}a_{s}\exp\left(-\frac{u_{p m}}{k T}\right)\left[N_{s}(1-f)-N_{m}\right]}}\end{array}
$$
where $u_{p m}$ and ${u_{p l}}$ are the corresponding potential energy in adsorbed states. $q_{v x},q_{v y},q_{v z}$ are the vibrational partition functions in x, y, z direction s. ;@\$D  is the translational partition function. $\boldsymbol{q^{\prime}}_{m}$ is the partition function for mobile molecules without size correction, which is given as )′\$ $\begin{array}{r}{{q^{\prime}}_{m}=\left(\frac{2\pi m k T}{h^{2}}\right)q_{v z}a_{s}\exp{\left(-\frac{u_{p m}}{k T}\right)}.}\end{array}$
The molecular distribution over mobile and localized regions can be derived as:35a
$$
\frac{\theta_{m}}{(1-f-\theta_{m})q_{\ m}^{\prime}}\exp\left(\frac{\theta_{m}}{1-f-\theta_{m}}\right)=\frac{\theta_{l}}{(f-\theta_{l})q_{l}}
$$
In order to present Eq.[6] in a figure, the relative value of $\boldsymbol{q^{\prime}}_{m}$ and $q_{l}$ and possible $f$ have to be estimated from Eq.[4] and $\left[5\right]$ . A $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecule has a mass $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ of $2.66\mathrm{X10}^{-}$ $^{26}\ \mathrm{kg}$ and a cross-sectional area36 $\left(\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{s}}\right)$ of $\mathbf{o.164nm}^{2}$ . For simplicity, all vibrational partition functions were assumed to be unity and $u_{p m}=u_{p l}$ . Substituting into Eq. [4] and [5], ${q^{\prime}}_{m}/q_{l}=254$ was obtained. The distributive ratios of the localized $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(\theta_{l}/\theta)$ were graphically depicted with an assumption of $q_{\ m}^{\prime}/q_{l}=10$ , 30, 100, 254 in Figure 8a, and $\scriptstyle f=0.3$ , 0.5, 0.7, 0.9 in Figure 8b, respectively. All the curves possess a maximum before full coverage $(\theta=1.0),$ ).
We selected $q_{\ m}^{\prime}/q_{l}=30$ and $f=\mathbf{o.}5$ as representative (blue trace in Figure 8c). It is expected that $\theta_{l}/\theta$ approaches $f$ with a positive slope; nevertheless, an interesting range showing $\theta_{l}/\theta>f$ occurs at near $85\%$ of the coverage $\left(\theta\approx0.85\right)$ , and thus a negative slope is necessary to complete full coverage. One of the possibilities for the negative slope of $\frac{\Delta\theta_{l}/\theta}{\Delta\theta}$ is molecular transport from a localized area to a mobile area. If the open metal sites and the nearby window pockets are regarded as a localized part and a mobile part for adsorbates respectively, the localized-to-mobile model will match our experimental observations of kinetic transition in $\mathrm{PCN-}30\mathrm{X}$ and PCN-12. However, if both the open metal sites and the window sites are localized parts, the exponential term in Eq.[6] will be absent, resulting in a monotonic increasing function without any negative slopes in Figure 8c (red trace). That also means no molecular transport will happen under the localized-to-localized assumption.
In summary, the sequential measurements of in-situ powder X-ray diffraction give insight into gas kinetic transitions occurring between open metal sites and the neighboring structural spaces. In spite of the fact that the open metal sites possess potential wells and significant exothermic enthalpy, there is another possibility to reach minimum Gibbs energy for the adsorption process by increasing entropy. In this section, we experimentally visualized the abstract concept of this entropy-driven process. The localized-to-mobile mechanism was proposed for the cause of gas kinetic transition, supporting the observations of molecular transport from one site to another.
